WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.630
In 2003, Elon Musk and Harold Rosen, the engineer
who invented the geostationary satellite,

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.510
had lunch at a seafood restaurant near SpaceX
headquarters in LA.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.849
Harold brought along Jeffrey Brian Straubel,
better known as JB Straubel.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:17.300
Together, they tried to convince Elon to build
an electric plane.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.990
Elon wasn’t interested, but then, the conversation
turned to electric cars.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.750
JB had spent his time at Stanford building
and racing solar-powered cars that relied

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.699
on lithium-ion batteries.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:30.910
He wondered what would happen if you packed
thousands of them together.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.090
He believed he was onto something big.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.610
No one saw the potential.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.610
Except Elon…who, having made a fortune when
his X.com merged with PayPal, which was bought

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.350
by eBay, offered JB $10,000 to support his
side project of developing a new battery solution

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:45.450
for electric cars.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.190
“If we hadn’t had lunch in 2003, Tesla
wouldn’t exist, basically.”

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:55.190
JB introduced Elon to his friends at AC Propulsion,
who had built the tzero.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:00.410
Elon fell in love with the electric sports
car that could go 0 to 60 in under five seconds.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.820
He felt it could change people's perception
of EVs, which were seen as uninteresting at

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:05.820
the time.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.720
Despite Elon’s offer to fund commercializing
the vehicle, the AC Propulsion guys weren’t

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.259
interested, prompting JB to recall in Ashlee
Vance’s biography on Elon Musk, “...they

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:18.009
were sort of hopeless at business and refused
to do it.”

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:19.009
(page 99)

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:21.040
So, Elon set out to build his own tzero.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.079
AC Propulsion’s head Tom Gage mentioned
there were three other engineers who were

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:27.650
interested in building an electric car.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.119
Martin Eberhard and Mark Tarpenning incorporated
Tesla Motors in 2003.

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:34.159
Ian Wright joined a few months later.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:39.369
Elon invested $6.5 million in Tesla initially
and became the largest shareholder and chairman

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:40.950
of the company’s board.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.820
Not long after Elon joined Tesla, he hired
JB as an engineer for $95,000 a year.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:46.820
(May 2004)

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.880
He has said the worst decision of his career
was to get involved with so many partners

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.430
and not start Tesla with just JB.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:58.620
Elon respected JB for his immense engineering
talent, which he honed as a kid growing up

00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.950
in Wisconsin.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.020
When JB was thirteen, he hauled home an old
golf cart from the dump and got it working

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.520
again by rebuilding the electric motor.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.750
A lab accident in high school left a two-inch
scar on his left cheek.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.489
He once converted his Porsche into an electric
vehicle.

00:02:13.489 --> 00:02:17.910
At Tesla, the most complicated task was building
the battery pack.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.760
We’ve spent the majority of our engineering
resources on how to make a battery pack work

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.160
well, be long lived, and provide this kind
of range and acceleration in an automotive

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.350
environment at a cost that’s competitive
with vehicles on the road today.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.100
The innovation came with significant challenges.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.470
The batteries could explode if they got too
hot.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.220
If a single cell overheated, it could cause
a domino effect, triggering neighboring cells

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.260
to overheat, potentially sparking a fire.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.260
(show broll of firecrackers).

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.260
And JB was packing thousands of them together.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.520
After working on the problem for months, he
and his team engineered a sophisticated thermal

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.780
management system designed to keep the battery
pack within a safe temperature range.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.000
They also developed software to monitor the
state of each cell in the battery pack to

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.220
detect any sign of a problem.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.620
JB was the most important member of the technical
team and got promoted to Chief Technology

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.720
Officer a year later.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.690
In addition to his engineering prowess, JB
had another invaluable skill.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.700
He was the calm mediator between Elon and
the rest of Tesla’s employees.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.010
The go-to person to pass along messages to
the team.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.010
In a conversation with Ashlee Vance, JB confided:

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:26.010
“Elon is incredibly difficult to work for,
but it’s mostly because he’s so passionate.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.590
It seems like people can get afraid of him
and paralyzed in a weird way.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.129
I try to help everyone to understand what
his goals and visions are…”

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:38.110
JB happily worked in the shadows, never seeking
the limelight.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:41.800
All he cared about was pushing the electric
vehicle revolution forward.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.030
I think five, 10 years from today, we’re
going to see an even more rapid shift than

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.090
people are predicting toward all-electric
vehicles

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.090
But the journey was far from smooth.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:57.390
Tesla’s first car, the Roadster, which featured
about 7,000 battery cells packed together,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.720
went way over budget and fell behind schedule.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.359
The company nearly went bankrupt in 2008.

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:08.950
After barely squeaking by, Tesla set its sights
on its next challenge, a luxury sedan.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:13.090
JB advocated for integrating charging capabilities
into the Model S.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.139
This was prior to the Supercharger network
being fully developed.

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:21.299
He favored this approach over battery-swapping,
which Elon seriously considered

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:22.299
Clip What if people do want to switch out
their battery pack?

00:04:22.299 --> 00:04:27.449
He even promoted it at an event in 2013, demonstrating
that it took less time to swap out a battery

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:31.740
than to put a tank of gas in a conventional
car.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.990
However, battery swapping was not the best
option, given the high cost of building battery-swapping

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.860
stations and the technical complexity.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.330
The Supercharger network has proved to be
the right strategy, offering Tesla a huge

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.430
competitive advantage.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:52.889
Tesla has now opened up its charging network
for other automakers to use.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:05:00.300
JB helped transform Tesla into a mass-market
car maker.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.840
He spearheaded the construction and operation
of Tesla’s first mega battery factory, Gigafactory

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.020
Nevada, aimed at boosting battery production
for the Model 3.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.120
This factory is going to be about 6 million
square feet of ground area.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.300
We can fit about 100 747s parked in the footprint
of this building.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:19.669
Not only that, we can fit 50 billion hamsters.

00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:25.150
Today, Teslas go much further than many other
electric cars thanks to its high-powered battery

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:26.150
tech.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:31.980
In 2019, JB stepped down as Chief Technology
Officer and transitioned to an advisory role,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.610
handing over the reins to his protégé Drew
Baglino.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:40.580
I'd like to thank JB for his fundamental role
in creating and building Tesla.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.220
Thank you, JB.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.040
It's been quite an adventurous 16 years.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:50.300
When he reflected on his departure, Straubel
said: “It has been a really tough decision

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.610
because I feel like I’m letting a lot of
people down.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.310
But, also, you have to live life.”

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.800
He was raising young twin boys with his wife
Boryana, whom he met at Tesla when she worked

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.930
in human resources.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.980
But…tragedy would strike his family.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.130
On the morning of June 19, 2021, Boryana was
biking in the cycling lane on a rural Nevada

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.569
road when a car traveling in the opposite
direction crossed over to her side of the

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:17.520
road and struck her, killing her.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.810
She was 38 years old.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:25.319
Boryana was an advocate of environmental sustainability
and founded a jewelry company that used recycled

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:27.180
metals for its designs.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:32.310
JB has continued to honor his wife's legacy
through his work on sustainability.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:38.120
After leaving Tesla, he launched Redwood Materials,
a startup that recycles lithium-ion batteries,

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.690
including scrap from the gigafactory, to create
new battery materials.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:48.020
The mission is not only to avoid waste but
to disrupt traditional mining by recycling

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.490
elements like nickel, cobalt, and lithium
for use for automakers.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:57.180
This could lower EV prices by reducing the
cost of battery packs, one of the most expensive

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.419
components in an electric car.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:05.870
JB was the heart of Tesla from the onset…the
steady hand behind the larger-than-life CEO.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.490
And now, he’s back.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.889
JB rejoined Tesla as a member of its board
of directors in the spring of 2023.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:16.830
It will be interesting to see what else he
has to offer Tesla and the entire EV industry

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.190
in the years ahead.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:23.889
JB Straubel’s talent as an engineer has
helped revolutionize the electric car industry.

